<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huhanye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">submitted <a href="/years/51-bce">51 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huhanye was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>, or supreme ruler, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> during the succession conflicts of the mid-first century BCE. In 51 BCE he submitted to Han suzerainty, travelling in person to the Han court in exchange for protection and trade privileges. His submission split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and marked a turning point in the decline of the Xiongnu Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huhanye.jpg" alt="Huhanye" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Postmann Michael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hsiung-nu-Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Huhanye was born Jihoushan, a son of Xulüquanqu Chanyu. In 59 BCE he rebelled with the help of Wushanmu, and the ruling chanyu, Woyanqudi, soon took his own life, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> divided among warring factions. By 55 BCE only two claimants remained, Huhanye and his brother Zhizhi Chanyu, and the two fought each other for the leadership of the confederacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhizhi">Zhizhi</a>, Huhanye fled south, submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to visit Emperor Xuan, who allowed his people to settle in the Yinshan area. Zhizhi also submitted to the Han the following year, but in 48 BCE, seeing that the court favoured his brother, he declared his independence and moved west to attack Fergana and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>. Huhanye moved back north in 43 BCE, and when Zhizhi was killed by the Han at the Battle of Zhizhi in 36 BCE he became the uncontested leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>.</p><p>In 33 BCE Huhanye came again to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> under the tributary arrangements between the two governments and asked to become an imperial son-in-law. Unwilling to give him a real princess, Emperor Yuan had a palace girl chosen instead, and the lady-in-waiting Wang Zhaojun volunteered. She is counted among the Four Beauties of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, beside Xi Shi, Diaochan and Yang Guifei.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wang Zhaojun became a favourite of Huhanye and bore him two sons, of whom only Yituzhiyashi is recorded as surviving, and two daughters; one of them, Yun, was created Princess Yimuo and later became a powerful figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> politics. Huhanye died in 31 BCE. His sons by several wives followed him in turn as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>, among them Fuzhulei Ruodi, Souxie, Juya and Wuzhuliu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huhanye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the civil war of 60–53 BCE the chanyu <b>Huhanye</b> moved south and submitted to the Han, using Chinese support against his brother Zhizhi; the Han reduced the Xiongnu from a &quot;brotherly state&quot; to an &quot;outer vassal&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 51 BCE the chanyu <b>Huhanye</b> travelled in person to the Han court at Chang'an and acknowledged Han overlordship in return for protection and gifts, while his brother and rival Zhizhi withdrew westward, where he was killed by a Han expedition in 36 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In a war between rival chanyus that began in 60 BCE, <b>Huhanye</b> accepted Han overlordship and paid homage in person at Chang’an in 51 BCE; in 48 CE the Xiongnu split for good into southern and northern realms.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
